<article class="ed-guide bg-canvas py-12 md:py-16" aria-label="Before an assessment,"><div class="ed-layout">
{{ contents([{"id": "start", "title": "Find your starting point"}, {"id": "section-1", "title": "Describe one task that matters"}, {"id": "section-2", "title": "Copy this preparation checklist"}, {"id": "section-3", "title": "Gather observations without changing the evidence"}, {"id": "section-4", "title": "Ask for a decision you can understand"}, {"id": "related", "title": "What to read next"}]) }}
<div class="ed-reading guide-reading text-lg">
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">You do not need a technical diagnosis before asking for help. A few precise notes about the work you are trying to do can make the conversation more useful than saying the computer is “old” or “terrible”.</p>
<section class="ed-start" id="start" aria-labelledby="start-heading"><p class="ed-label">Start here</p><h2 id="start-heading">Where should your notes begin?</h2><p>Choose the part of your everyday work that is being interrupted.</p><div class="ed-choices">
{{ choice("Getting started", "Describe the wait before the computer becomes usable.", "#section-1", "startup", "", "", "Read this part") }}
{{ choice("Using a program", "Name the task and what you expected to happen.", "#section-1", "applications", "", "", "Read this part") }}
{{ choice("Going online", "Say whether the waiting is mainly on websites or calls.", "#section-1", "online", "", "", "Read this part") }}
</div></section>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="section-1"><span aria-hidden="true">01</span>Describe one task that matters</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Choose something ordinary: opening an application, finding a file already on the computer or getting ready for a video call. Write down where the waiting begins and what you expected to happen.</p>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">For example: “After signing in, I wait before the desktop becomes usable” describes a different experience from “The desktop works, but one website takes ages.” Use the <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="/guides/computer-slow-startup/">startup guide</a> or <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="/guides/slow-computer-or-internet/">computer-versus-internet guide</a> if you need help separating those observations.</p>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="section-2"><span aria-hidden="true">02</span>Copy this preparation checklist</h2>
<ul class="mb-6 list-disc space-y-3 pl-6 text-muted">
<li><strong>Device:</strong> the model if you know it; “Windows laptop” is a useful start.</li>
<li><strong>Task:</strong> what you were trying to do when it felt slow.</li>
<li><strong>Timing:</strong> roughly how long you waited and how often it happens.</li>
<li><strong>History:</strong> when it started and what changed around that time.</li>
<li><strong>Messages:</strong> the wording of any warning, without private account details.</li>
<li><strong>Importance:</strong> what you need the computer to handle reliably.</li>
<li><strong>Files:</strong> whether important files are accessible and whether you have a usable backup.</li>
<li><strong>Previous work:</strong> what has already been tried and whether it helped.</li>
</ul>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Leave unknown items blank. Approximate timings are fine; this is not a benchmark competition.</p>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="section-3"><span aria-hidden="true">03</span>Gather observations without changing the evidence</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">If you want to look at built-in performance information, our <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="/guides/windows-performance-observations/">Windows observation guide</a> explains an observation-only approach. You do not need to install software to complete this checklist.</p>
{% call note("care", "Take care", "Preserve the starting condition") %}<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Avoid a last-minute cleanup that deletes unfamiliar files or changes several settings. If the computer will not start, files are inaccessible, or it asks for unfamiliar recovery information, say that first. That may change the starting scope. Do not reset the computer to make it ready for an assessment.</p>{% endcall %}
{% call note("care", "Take care", "Keep credentials in your control") %}<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Keep passwords and recovery codes out of your notes and enquiry. You can unlock your own device when appropriate without handing over a written password list.</p>{% endcall %}
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="section-4"><span aria-hidden="true">04</span>Ask for a decision you can understand</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Useful questions for the assessment include:</p>
<ol class="mb-6 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6 text-muted">
<li>What did you find, and what evidence supports it?</li>
<li>What can be improved within the agreed visit?</li>
<li>What remains uncertain or needs separate work?</li>
<li>Would a repair, upgrade or replacement fit what I actually do?</li>
<li>What should I keep, back up or prepare before the next step?</li>
</ol>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">The <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="/guides/slow-computer-fix-upgrade-replace/">fix, upgrade or replace pillar</a> puts those choices in context. Replacement is not an automatic consequence of age or one slow task.</p>


